<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Overall Performance</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To answer <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1</span>, we conduct a systematic evaluation of MoT across three fundamental graph learning tasks (node classification, edge classification, and graph classification) under two distinct learning paradigms: (1) supervised fine-tuning and (2) few-shot/zero-shot transfer.
We compare MoT with three categories of baselines: traditional supervised GNN (GCN), unsupervised GNNs (GraphMAE, GIANT), and GFMs (GFT, OFA, SAMGPT, UniGraph).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Two Sides of the Same Optimization Coin: Model Degradation and Representation Collapse in Graph Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> demonstrates MoT variants’ strong performance in supervised scenarios, where even the lightest variant MoT-st-tiny exceeds all baseline models.
Compared to traditional GNNs, MoT benefits from large-scale pre-training.
Compared to existing graph foundation models, MoT achieves superior generalization through its dual-tinker architecture:
➀ the information tinker dynamically fuses edge semantics to prevent model degradation, while
➁ the regularization tinker enforces geometric constraints via contrastive alignment to avert representation collapse.
Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Two Sides of the Same Optimization Coin: Model Degradation and Representation Collapse in Graph Foundation Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> highlights MoT’s exceptional adaptability in data-scarce scenarios, which consistently outperforms existing methods across few-shot and zero-shot learning settings.
These results stems from the synergistic dual-tinker architecture, where the Mixture-of-Codebooks intrinsically captures transferable domain invariances, while the regularization tinker enforces geometric consistency in latent representations.
Such design achieves enhanced generalization by universalizing structural patterns across domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, MoT demonstrates remarkable adherence to GFM scaling laws across all evaluation dimensions.
As model scale increases from tiny to large variants, we observe consistent performance improvements while maintaining stable variance patterns.
MoT-st-large achieves peak performance on 6 of 8 fine-tuning tasks and 15 of 16 few-shot settings, though notably underperforms MoT-st-base in certain cases.
While MoT variants successfully follow scaling law, several critical issues remain unaddressed.
For instance, insufficient pre-training data may lead to suboptimal parameter utilization and diminished performance returns, highlighting substantial room for future research.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
